"[2609.06022] Fast Evaluation of Polynomials with Rational Preprocessing (https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.06022)\nciteturn3view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.06022\",\"lineno\":20}); Total lines: 161\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Data Structures and Algorithms\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 5 Sep 2026]\nL9: # Title:Fast Evaluation of Polynomials with Rational Preprocessing\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【5†Thomas D. Ahle】, 【6†Jakob B. T. Knudsen】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Fast Evaluation of Polynomials with Rational Preprocessing, by Thomas D. Ahle and Jakob B. T. Knudsen\nL14: \nL15: 【7†View PDF】 【8†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:Horner's rule evaluates a monic degree-$n$ polynomial using $n-1$ multiplications. We show that with rational preprocessing of the coefficients, any such polynomial can be evaluated using only $\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor + 1$ multiplications over fields of characteristic zero or of characteristic $p>n$. This resolves the multiplication side of a conjecture of Rabin and Winograd (Comm. Pure Appl.\nL17: Math 1972), who achieved $n/2 + 2\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil$ multiplications and conjectured the logarithmic overhead was necessary.\nL18: > We show that this multiplication count can't be beaten in general, proving that three multiplications do not suffice for degree~$6$. This strengthens the lower bound of Pan (STOC 1978), who proved a tight bound for general, complex preprocessing.\nL19: > In characteristic~2, for every $n>1$ and every finite field of size at least $2n$, we prove that an $n$-multiplication chain cannot parametrize all value vectors at $2n$ distinct evaluation points, even with arbitrary preprocessing. We give $\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor + 1$ multiplication schedules over characteristic~2, each with an explicit inverse, for every odd degree $n\\le 25$ and conjecture that this is possible for all $n$.\nL20: > We also give an injective polynomial construction for universal hashing that uses $N$ multiplications to hash $2N$ values with a single random key. This improves the best previous construction by Daniel J. Bernstein (【9†this http URL†cryp.to】).\nL21: Subjects:  | Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)\nL22: Cite as:  | 【10†arXiv:2609.06022】 [cs.DS]\nL23:    | (or 【11†arXiv:2609.06022v1】 [cs.DS] for this version)\nL24:    | 【12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2609.06022†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite (pending registration)\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Thomas D. Ahle [【13†view email】]\nL28: [v1] Sat, 5 Sep 2026 10:49:57 UTC (456 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Fast Evaluation of Polynomials with Rational Preprocessing, by Thomas D. Ahle and Jakob B. T. Knudsen\nL35: \nL36:   * 【7†View PDF】\nL37:   * 【8†HTML (experimental)】\nL38:   * 【14†TeX Source】\nL39: \nL40: 【15†view license†creativecommons.org】\nL41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.DS\nL44: \nL45: 【16†< prev】   |   【17†next >】\nL46: \nL47: 【18†new】 | 【19†recent】 | 【20†2026-09】\nL48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: 【21†cs】\nL52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * 【22†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu】\nL56:   * 【23†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL57:   * 【24†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org】\nL58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: ### Bookmark\nL70: \nL71: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL72: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL75: \nL76: Bibliographic Explorer (【25†What is the Explorer?†info.arxi